<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Campania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Campania is a region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> as its capital. Its fertile volcanic plain drew Greek colonists, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> and Samnites, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> won control of it in the 4th century BCE. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was divided among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, Capua and Salerno and the coastal duchies, until the Normans united it in the 12th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/campania.jpg" alt="Campania" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campania_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Etruscan culture flourished in three confederacies of cities: in Etruria itself, in the Po Valley and in <b>Campania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome recovered quickly, built the Servian Wall of Grotta Oscura tufa and resumed its expansion in Latium and <b>Campania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 6th century BCE Etruscan power reached from the Po valley, where Felsina (the later Bologna), the new town of Marzabotto and the Adriatic port of Spina flourished, to <b>Campania</b>, around Capua.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Most come from Sardinia, but a sizeable minority were found in Etruscan tombs at Vulci, Vetulonia and Populonia, in <b>Campania</b> and at the Greek colony of Crotone.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nuragic-civilisation">Nuragic civilisation · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Lombard warriors under Zotto established themselves at Beneventum in <b>Campania</b> around 571, perhaps first as soldiers in Byzantine pay.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-benevento">Duchy of Benevento</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hirpini lived in the mountains of modern Irpinia, east of <b>Campania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hirpini">Hirpini</a></li></ul></section>
